 r"I am writing to apply for the Graduate Data Scientist position at Wise. I am currently completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, after graduating from Imperial College London with a BSc in Biological Sciences. Across my academic and professional experience I have built predictive and causal models on large datasets, evaluated machine learning and language model systems against a clear standard, and explained the results to the people who had to act on them. This role is close to what I have actually done: taking a messy question, deciding how to measure it, and building the model or test that answers it.",

 r"What attracts me most to Wise is that data scientists are trusted to decide what to work on. The posting describes graduates proposing their own ideas in quarterly planning rather than waiting to be told what to build, and that is how I work best. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved. I also like that the work spans so many teams, from Marketing to Treasury and Fraud. I have changed domains a few times already, and each move has made me quicker at learning enough about a new area to ask useful questions in it.",

 r"I currently work as a Data Analyst at TikTok Shop UK, where I mine merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python to find the growth and operations drivers behind the UK marketplace, and build KPI frameworks and dashboards that e-commerce teams use to set their targets. I also built an AI agent with Claude Code that automates repetitive steps of our analytics workflow, and it has been adopted by my own team and by partner teams. Building it meant understanding an existing workflow in detail, deciding which steps to automate, and then supporting colleagues as they began to rely on it.",

 r"Before that I was an Applied AI Engineer at DS Digital Technology, working for a pharmaceutical client on patient-facing medical answers. I built a Python pipeline that called six LLM APIs with retrieval and multi-step workflows, producing and scoring more than 2,400 responses, and designed the 246-question benchmark and six-dimension rubric that the client adopted as its acceptance standard. I also helped build a medically reviewed knowledge graph and ran blinded A/B tests to check whether it actually improved answer quality. Agreeing what a good answer looked like before running anything turned out to be the most useful part of the project, and it is the habit I would bring to model work at Wise.",

 r"My MSc dissertation is where most of my modelling sits. I built cardiovascular risk models on 458,840 UK Biobank participants, comparing logistic regression with three tree ensembles and paying as much attention to calibration as to AUC. I then used doubly robust causal forests on 71,428 participants to estimate how lifestyle changes affect risk, with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding and external validation in a second cohort, and delivered the results as a Streamlit app. Outside my degree I built Jobwinner, a multi-agent job search assistant that reasons over job postings and grounds its recommendations in live web search.",

 r"At Ipsos Strategy3 I analysed data from more than 80 healthcare professional interviews, turning their answers into variables that could be compared, and presented the findings to client stakeholders. That is where I learned to present a finding to an audience who wants the implication rather than the method.",

 r"I would bring Wise strong Python and SQL, hands-on experience with supervised models, causal inference and language model workflows, and the habit of checking whether something worked before recommending it.",

